  "reviews": [
    {
      "author": "MovieGuys",
      "content": "\"Ghost Train\" is a rather uneven horror thriller from South Korea. \r\n\r\nCreative ideas, sit side by side, with mediocre one's, that show little imaginative investment. The notion of a woman turning into a flower is hardly inspiring, whilst the soft drink dispenser story, is actually quite intriguing.\r\n\r\nThis pattern repeats itself over the space of this film, with an ending that's shows a dash of flair but is also wholly predictable.\r\n\r\nCharacterisations share a similar fate. They feel like they can't quite rise above the inconsistencies, in the stories told. This is balanced out somewhat, by quality acting, from the cast.\r\n\r\nIn summary, \"Ghost Train\" is a mixed bag embracing both imagination and mediocrity, in equal measure. An okay horror thriller watch.",
      "rating": 6,
      "createdAt": "2025-09-04T00:15:30Z"
    },
    {
      "author": "Alunauwie",
      "content": "Ghost Train weaves interconnected mystery stories around Da Kyeong, a YouTuber whose jealousy and obsession gradually blur her moral compass. While the layered narrative and multiple cases sometimes overshadow deeper exploration of motive, the film maintains steady tension through atmosphere, strong performances, and emotional consequences rather than relying solely on jump scares. In the end, it works as both a supernatural thriller and a reflection on ambition and envy, leaving a lingering sense of unease.\r\n\r\nRead the full review here: (Indonesian version : alunauwie.com) and  (English version : uwiepuspita.com)",
      "rating": 7,
      "createdAt": "2026-02-28T04:37:52Z"
    }
  ],
